; smf-smpte — a wall-clock division, and the tempo event nobody believes.
;
; 25 frames per second, 40 ticks per frame: OpenMPT's `frames ·
; subframes / 2` makes that a 500-pulse grid, and the file's `set-tempo`
; is kept and recorded as ignored (`smpte_tempo_ignored`, plan 03).
;
; No `(roundtrip)`: the canonical writer's division is ALWAYS pulses per
; quarter (plan 05, "The canonical writer"), so `write(read(bytes))`
; deliberately differs from these bytes in exactly the two division bytes
; — rung 1 is a gate on files the writer produced, and this one is
; authored to be one it never would. What a round trip WOULD preserve
; here, event for event, is gated by the canonical cases beside it.
;
; `(write)` is what stands in for it: the canonical writer's output for
; this file, hashed — the SMPTE file written back onto the 500-pulse grid
; its division converted to, which is the rule plan 05 states.
(read)
(write)
